From 37eaf15849c22bd83d16d531c69f0d9e3a582f37 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: AMDNR <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 3 Oct 2026 19:35:43 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 3/3] fix(scaling): a stop nobody asked for no longer turns
 scaling off for good

A player on an RX 9070 XT reported that taking a screenshot during a session
turns the upscaler off permanently: it never comes back while they stay in the
same game.

Two pieces make that permanent. ScalingService::_CheckForegroundTimer_Tick
records every foreground window it has seen in _hwndChecked, including the
source window on every tick of a healthy run, and returns early for a window it
has already checked. _ScalingRuntime_StateChanged then sets the same mark again
when scaling ends with the source in front, for the one case the upstream
comment names: the player just stopped scaling with the hotkey or the toolbar,
so do not restart it under their hand. Nothing distinguished that from a stop
the player never asked for. A screenshot overlay takes the foreground, the 3D
game mode check rejects it (ScalingWindow.cpp:1737, which every PLAY ANYWHERE
profile enables), scaling stops, the game is in front again - and the automatic
check stays dead for as long as the game stays in front.

Carry why a run ended with the state. ScalingRuntime::_State publishes the
cause before the notification, on the thread that publishes it, so the handler
reads a cause that belongs to that notification; the hotkey, the toolbar close
button and the task-switch option mark their stops as the player's. A stop
nobody asked for - lost capture, a transient window above the source, a runtime
fault - now releases the source window instead, so automatic scaling arms
itself again and the picture returns on its own. Releasing is the part that
matters: leaving the mark alone changes nothing, the healthy run had already
set it.

It is bounded: three automatic restarts in a row per window and then the window
is left alone, so a source that fails for a real reason cannot thrash. A run
that lasts past the reset window, or a start the player makes themselves,
clears the tally. One line per stop now says whether the player asked for it,
which window was being scaled and what was in front when it ended.

A second report (Ghost Recon Breakpoint, 2560x1440) showed the other half.
There the source was hidden rather than losing the foreground, which takes the
reposition route and restarts itself in 126 ms - but the restart measured the
window as 4,28,2556,1436 (2552x1408) where it had been 0,0,2560,1440: a border
and a title bar the game was still in the middle of dropping after applying new
graphics settings. ScalingRuntime's reposition poll restarted on the first look
where the window was visible, with nothing asking whether it had finished
changing, and SrcTracker::UpdateState watches only the window rect and the
maximized flag, so a frame change that keeps the window rect was never noticed
and the wrong crop lasted the whole session: a shifted, mis-sized picture and a
cursor mapping off by the same amount.

Wait for the window's shape - its rect and the thickness of its non-client area
- to stop changing before measuring it, capped at 400 ms so a window that never
settles is still scaled. And treat a change in that thickness during a run like
a size change, so a measurement that still came out wrong corrects itself
instead of lasting until the player restarts.

tests/Run-AutoScaleRearmTests.ps1 extracts the latch, the foreground check, the
state publication and the settle gate. It covers the deliberate stop keeping
its behaviour, the involuntary stop releasing the window, the bound and its
three reset paths, a changing frame restarting the settle wait, the 400 ms cap
and unreadable geometry.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
